The error you're encountering is due to an input mismatch exception being thrown when trying to read an integer from the user using a Scanner object. This can happen if the user enters a non-numeric value or if there's an issue with the input stream.

To resolve this issue, you can add a try-catch block around the code that reads the integer input and handle the InputMismatchException appropriately. Here's an example of how you can modify your code:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        try {
            System.out.print("Enter the first number: ");
            int num1 = scanner.nextInt();

            System.out.print("Enter the second number: ");
            int num2 = scanner.nextInt();

            // Perform calculations or other operations with num1 and num2
        } catch (InputMismatchException e) {
            System.out.println("Invalid input. Please enter a valid numeric value.");
            scanner.nextLine(); // Consume any remaining input on the line
        }
    }
}
```

In this modified code, we wrap the code that reads the integer inputs in a try-catch block. If an InputMismatchException is thrown, we catch it and display an error message to the user. We then consume any remaining input on the line using the `scanner.nextLine()` method to prevent any further issues.

With these changes, your program should be able to handle invalid input more gracefully and avoid crashing with an InputMismatchException.

