To retrieve the username from the JWT token in ASP.NET C# WebAPI, you can use the `JwtSecurityTokenHandler` class to parse the token and extract the username claim. Here's an example:
```csharp
using System;
using System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt;
using System.Linq;
using System.Security.Claims;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

namespace WebApiExample.Controllers
{
    [Authorize]
    [Route("[controller]")]
    public class ValuesController : ControllerBase
    {
        [HttpGet]
        public ActionResult<string> Get()
        {
            var token = HttpContext.Request.Headers["Authorization"].FirstOrDefault()?.ToString()?.Substring("Bearer ".Length);
            if (token == null)
            {
                return BadRequest("No token found");
            }

            var tokenHandler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
            var key = Encoding.ASCII.Parse("your_secret_key");
            var tokenValidationParameters = new TokenValidationParameters
            {
                IssuerSigningKey = key,
                ValidateIssuer = false,
                ValidateAudience = false,
                ValidateLifetime = true,
                ClockSkew = TimeSpan.Zero
            };

            var securityToken = tokenHandler.ReadJwtToken(token, tokenValidationParameters);
            var usernameClaim = securityToken.Claims.SingleOrDefault(c => c.Type == "http://schemas.microsoft.com/identity/claims/username")?.Value;

            if (usernameClaim == null)
            {
                return BadRequest("No username claim found");
            }

            return Ok($"Hello, {usernameClaim}!");
        }
    }
}
```
Replace `"your_secret_key"` with your secret key used for signing the JWT token. This code will retrieve the username claim from the JWT token and return a greeting message with the user's name.

Inner functions and outer functions refer to two types of function definitions in programming. Let's break down each type:

1. Inner functions: These are functions that are defined within another function. They have access to the local variables and parameters of the enclosing function, as well as any global variables. Inner functions can be called from both within and outside their enclosing function.

Example:
```javascript
function outerFunction() {
  let x = 10;
  function innerFunction() {
    console.log(x); // Accesses the local variable 'x' of the enclosing function
  }
  innerFunction();
}
outerFunction();
```
In this example, `innerFunction` is an inner function because it is defined within `outerFunction`. It has access to `x`, which is a local variable of `outerFunction`.

2. Outer functions: These are functions that are defined at the top level of a program or module, and are not nested inside another function. They have access to global variables and parameters, but do not have direct access to local variables of inner functions. Outer functions can only be called from outside their defining scope.

Example:
```javascript
function outerFunction() {
  let x = 10;
}
function innerFunction() {
  console.log(x); // Throws a reference error since it cannot access 'x' (a local variable of outerFunction)
}
outerFunction();
```
In this example, `innerFunction` is an outer function because it is defined at the top level of the program and is not nested inside another function. It tries to access `x`, which is a local variable of `outerFunction`, but since it is an outer function, it cannot directly access it. This results in a reference error.

In summary, inner functions can access the local variables and parameters of their enclosing function, while outer functions cannot. Inner functions can be called from both within and outside their enclosing function, while outer functions can only be called from outside their defining scope.

