It seems like the issue is related to the 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header set by the server. When the server sets this header to '\*', it allows any origin to access the resource, including requests without the necessary authentication credentials.

To include the authorization header in your request, you can try setting the 'Access-Control-Allow-Credentials' header to 'true' on the server-side. This will allow the browser to send the authorization header with the request.

Alternatively, you can use a tool like CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) to manage the access control between different origins. CORS allows you to specify which origins are allowed to access your resource and what type of requests they can make.

Here's an example of how you can use CORS to allow cross-origin requests with authorization headers:
```
// Server-side (e.g., Node.js with Express)
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.use(function(req, res, next) {
  res.header("Access-Control-Allow-Origin", "*");
  res.header("Access-Control-Allow-Methods", "GET, POST, PUT, DELETE");
  res.header("Access-Control-Allow-Headers", "Authorization");
  next();
});

app.get('/api/data', function(req, res) {
  // Your API logic here
});

app.listen(3000);
```
In this example, we're using the `express` framework to create a simple API server. We're setting the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header to '\*' to allow any origin to access the resource. We're also setting the `Access-Control-Allow-Methods` header to 'GET, POST, PUT, DELETE' to allow all HTTP methods. Finally, we're setting the `Access-Control-Allow-Headers` header to 'Authorization' to allow the browser to send the authorization header with the request.

With these settings, you should be able to include the authorization header in your request when making cross-origin requests to this API server.

