To inject JavaScript into an existing webpage using your Chrome extension, you'll need to create a content script that will execute the JavaScript code when the page is loaded. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. Create a new file named `inject.js` in your extension folder (usually `chrome/extensions/your_extension_id/`) and add the following code:
```javascript
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  const script = document.createElement('script');
  script.src = chrome.runtime.getURL('your_script.js');
  document.body.appendChild(script);
});
```
Replace `your_script.js` with the name of your JavaScript file that contains the code you want to inject into the webpage.

2. Update your `manifest.json` file to include the `inject.js` file as a content script:
```json
{
  "manifest_version": 2,
  "name": "Your Extension Name",
  "version": "1.0",
  "description": "Your extension description",
  "browser_action": {
    "default_icon": "icon.png",
    "default_popup": "popup.html"
  },
  "permissions": ["activeTab"],
  "content_scripts": [
    {
      "matches": ["<all_urls>"],
      "js": ["inject.js"]
    }
  ]
}
```
This will tell Chrome to inject the `inject.js` file into all webpages the user visits while your extension is active.

3. Create your JavaScript file (e.g., `your_script.js`) and add the code you want to inject into the webpage. For example:
```javascript
console.log('Injected!');
```

4. Test your extension by loading a webpage in Chrome and activating your extension. You should see the console message "Injected!" in the developer console.

Remember to replace `your_script.js` with the name of your actual JavaScript file, and adjust the code in `inject.js` and `manifest.json` as needed for your specific use case.

