To prevent the planets from being too close together, you can add a simple check for distance between each planet before adding it to the list. You can do this by modifying the `generateRandomPlanets` function. Here's an updated version of the function:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    const tooClose = planets.some((p) =>
      p.position.some((coord, index) => {
        return Math.abs(coord - planet.position[index]) < minDistance
      })
    );

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  return planets;
};
```

In this version, we added a check inside the loop that checks if the new planet is too close to any existing planet using the `some()` method. If the planet is too close, we skip it and try again with a new position. We also added a limit to the number of attempts to avoid infinite loops.

With this change, the planets should be generated with enough space between them, preventing them from being too close together.

Here's a simple React utility that removes widows from text by breaking words at the end of a line and distributing them evenly across multiple lines:

```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ text }) => {
  const [lines, setLines] = useState([]);

  const handleTextChange = (event) => {
    const newText = event.target.value;
    const newLines = breakWidows(newText);
    setLines(newLines);
  };

  const breakWidows = (text) => {
    const words = text.split(' ');
    const lineWidth = 60; // Adjust this value based on your desired line width.
    const numLines = Math.ceil(words.length / lineWidth);

    const linesArray = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < numLines; i++) {
      const startIndex = i * lineWidth;
      const endIndex = Math.min(startIndex + lineWidth, words.length);
      const lineText = words.slice(startIndex, endIndex).join(' ');
      linesArray.push(lineText);
    }

    return linesArray;
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <textarea value={text} onChange={handleTextChange} />
      <ul>
        {lines.map((line, index) => (
          <li key={index}>{line}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

This component takes a `text` prop and displays it in a text area. As the user types, the component breaks the text into lines using the specified line width and displays each line in a list below the text area.

