Here's a PowerShell script that recursively checks folders for ACLs referring to SIDs in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain:

```powershell
# Define the folder path to start the search from
$startFolder = "C:\Path\To\Your\Start\Folder"

# Function to check if an ACL refers to a SID in the SIDHistory
function CheckSidInSIDHistory($sid) {
    $domain = (Get-ADDomain -Identity (Get-ADDefaultDomain)).Name
    $sidHistory = Get-ADUser -Filter {SID -eq $sid} -Properties SIDHistory | Select-Object -ExpandProperty SIDHistory

    return ($sidHistory -ne $null)
}

# Recursively check folders for ACLs referring to SIDs in the SIDHistory
function CheckFoldersForACLs($folderPath) {
    Get-ChildItem -Path $folderPath -Directory | ForEach-Object {
        $acl = Get-Acl -Path $_.FullName
        $accessRules = $acl.Access | Where-Object {$_.IdentityReference -match "(?i)$domain"}

        foreach ($accessRule in $accessRules) {
            $sid = $accessRule.IdentityReference
            if (CheckSidInSIDHistory($sid)) {
                Write-Host "ACL for folder $($_.FullName) references SID $sid, which is in the SIDHistory of a user or group in the domain."
            }
        }

        CheckFoldersForACLs($_.FullName)
    }
}

# Start the search
CheckFoldersForACLs($startFolder)
```

Replace `"C:\Path\To\Your\Start\Folder"` with the starting folder path you want to search from. This script will recursively check all folders and subfolders within the specified path for ACLs that reference SIDs in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain.
